     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, all nine claims in `spec.k` prove the
     6	partial correctness of the translated `compare_one` body for every pairing of
     7	the three permitted modeled input kinds:
     8	
     9	- arbitrary `Int` with `Int`, `Float`, or `str(IntSeq)`;
    10	- arbitrary `Float` with `Int`, `Float`, or `str(IntSeq)`; and
    11	- arbitrary finite modeled string code sequence with `Int`, `Float`, or another
    12	  arbitrary finite modeled string code sequence.
    13	
    14	These are symbolic, unbounded domains. They are not fixed examples, fixed
    15	string lengths, or bounded unrollings. The claims execute module loading,
    16	ordinary name lookup, argument evaluation, parameter binding, both
    17	`isinstance` calls, both optional `replace`/`float` conversions, both
    18	comparisons, the selected return, frame popping, and restoration of the
    19	control cells under the unmodified reference semantics.
    20	
    21	The observable return is:
    22	
    23	```text
    24	value(x) = x                                      for modeled Int/Float x
    25	value(str(C)) = decStrToF(replaceC(C, ',', '.'))  for modeled strings
    26	
    27	compare_one(a,b) =
    28	    a     if value(a) > value(b)
    29	    b     if not(value(a) > value(b)) and value(b) > value(a)
    30	    None  otherwise
    31	```
    32	
    33	For real numbers, trichotomy makes the final case exactly numeric equality.
    34	The two-direction formulation avoids the unavailable Haskell `FLOAT.eq` hook
    35	while preserving the HumanEval contract on its stated real-number domain.
    36	NaN is not a real number and is outside the source contract.
    37	
    38	This is a reachability proof of partial correctness. It is not a separate
    39	liveness theorem, although every modeled path in these straight-line claims
    40	does reach its stated result.
    41	
    42	## Formal claims and scope
    43	
    44	`spec.k` contains the following target claims:
    45	
    46	```text
    47	SPEC.int-int       SPEC.int-float       SPEC.float-int
    48	SPEC.float-float   SPEC.int-str         SPEC.str-int
    49	SPEC.float-str     SPEC.str-float       SPEC.str-str
    50	```
    51	
    52	Together they cover the full Cartesian product of the three contract types.
    53	Every claim starts from the exact initial MPY configuration, loads the exact
    54	module represented by `solution.mpy`, and calls `compare_one` with symbolic
    55	arguments. The final return is `expectedCompare(A, B)`. The environment,
    56	heap, heap counter, scope counter, call stack, return state, exception state,
    57	and exit code are constrained to their expected restored values. The final
    58	scope map is existential because loading the function intentionally adds its
    59	global closure and the contract observes the return rather than the module
    60	namespace.
    61	
    62	The program boundary is the complete translated `compare_one` function. There
    63	are no helper functions and no loops. The intended property is that the
    64	original argument having the greater numeric interpretation is returned in its
    65	original type, or `None` is returned when the two real values are equal.
    66	
    67	## Proof-extension inventory
    68	
    69	There are no operational bridges, invocation intercepts, execution
    70	accelerators, added algebraic lemmas, or auxiliary circularities.
    71	
    72	### `solutionModule()`
    73	
    74	- Class: definitional summary (a nullary syntactic abbreviation).
    75	- Semantic role: names the exact `Module(...)` constructor tree generated in
    76	  `solution.mpy`; fixed `#loadAll` and function-call rules still execute it.
    77	- Domain and context: the one nullary term, used only as the argument of
    78	  `#loadAll` in the nine claims.
    79	- State footprint: none. Its equation only constructs syntax.
    80	- Value/control influence: selects the program body executed by every claim.
    81	- Justification: direct execution of `solution.mpy` and execution of
    82	  `solutionModule()` produce byte-identical final KORE configurations.
    83	- Dependents: all nine target claims.
    84	- Validation: `cmp solution-direct.kore solution-named.kore` exits 0. A
    85	  material body mutation is independently rejected by
    86	  `SPEC-BODY-MUTATION.changed-body`.
    87	
    88	### `numericValue(Val)`
    89	
    90	- Class: definitional summary.
    91	- Semantic role: postcondition-only name for the numeric value constructed by
    92	  the program. It never rewrites an operational `<k>` term.
    93	- Complete domain used by the proof: `Int`, `Float`, and `str(IntSeq)`.
    94	- Equations: identity on `Int` and `Float`; on strings,
    95	  `decStrToF(replaceC(C, 44, 46))`.
    96	- Guard coverage and overlap: the three constructor domains are disjoint and
    97	  cover every use in the target claims. The symbol is deliberately not marked
    98	  `total` over unrelated `Val` constructors.
    99	- State footprint: none.
   100	- Value influence: supplies the two comparisons in the postcondition.
   101	- Justification: these equations are the normal forms produced by the fixed
   102	  assignment, `str.replace`, and `float(str)` rules.
   103	- Dependents: `expectedCompare` and all nine target claims.
   104	- Validation: fixed program execution closes all nine claims; concrete LLVM
   105	  tests exercise each type pairing.
   106	
   107	### `expectedCompare(Val, Val)`
   108	
   109	- Class: definitional summary of the requested result.
   110	- Semantic role: postcondition only; it does not replace function execution.
   111	- Complete used domain: pairs whose members are `Int`, `Float`, or
   112	  `str(IntSeq)`.
   113	- Equations: return `A` under the first greater-than guard; return `B` under
   114	  its negation and the reverse greater-than guard; otherwise return `noneV`.
   115	- Guard coverage and overlap: `P`, `not P and Q`, and
   116	  `not P and not Q` are exhaustive and pairwise disjoint.
   117	- State footprint: none.
   118	- Value influence: fixes the target return.
   119	- Justification: direct formalization of greater/or-reverse-greater/equal over
   120	  real numbers.
   121	- Dependents: all nine target claims.
   122	- Validation: all claims print `#Top`; the false result mutation and body
   123	  mutation both stop with the opposite concrete value visible in `<k>`.
   124	
   125	Because none of these extensions replaces fixed execution, operational-bridge
   126	context-containment and state-preservation obligations are not applicable.
   127	
